WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.020
Now that Avengers: Endgame is finally out
in theaters, directors Joe and Anthony Russo

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:08.120
have been able to freely expand on some of
the movie's plot details.

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:12.500
One of these, which came to light at a recent
Q&amp;A session for Endgame, has even hinted that

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.300
future Marvel movies could be the setting
for a major comeback.

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:19.560
According to Slashfilm, someone in attendance
at the Q&amp;A asked the Russos to run through

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.760
exactly what happened with the Infinity Stones
at the start of the movie.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:26.660
The directors confirmed that, three weeks
after his initial snap, Thanos utilized the

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:31.010
Infinity Stones once again but this time to
destroy them, preventing them from being used

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.000
to reverse his work.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.100
Thanos using the Stones to destroy themselves
was one of the major inciting incidents of

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.820
Endgame, but there's one little issue with
it: time doesn't function correctly without

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:40.320
them.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:44.160
While the heroes are snatching up the Infinity
Stones in the past, the Ancient One explains

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.940
to Professor Hulk that you can't just pluck
a Stone from a certain place in time and not

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.300
return it, as that would create a branch alternate
timeline overrun with disorder.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.780
Just...not the kind of disorder shown in Back
to the Future.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:55.840
Time travel!

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:01.540
“No.”

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.050
The Ancient One tells Hulk that the Infinity
Stones are "essential to the flow of time,"

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:09.690
and that they must be replaced at the exact
moment in time from which they are taken.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:13.720
So if the Stones are so important to preserving
a properly functioning timeline, why didn't

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.840
the universe devolve into complete chaos after
Thanos destroyed them?

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.540
Well, as the Russos explained, Thanos didn't
actually wipe the Infinity Stones out of existence

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.280
he just reduced them to atoms.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.520
The Russos indicated that this means the Stones
are still out there, floating around on "an

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:28.160
atomic level."

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.171
Though they may not be tangible or fit for
use in their current state, the Infinity Stones

00:01:32.171 --> 00:01:35.710
remain a part of the universe and will continue
to keep things in order.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.430
This would suggest that the Infinity Stones
could appear again in a future Marvel movie.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:43.360
Plenty of characters have wielded the Infinity
Gauntlet throughout Marvel's history so there's

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.930
a ton of source material for writers and filmmakers
to use if they wish to bring back the Stones.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:51.460
If anyone's going to be the key to the Infinity
Stones' return, there's a good chance it's

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:55.300
going to be Adam Warlock, who has been tied
to the Stones since their print debut.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.920
For some time, Adam was the primary bearer
of the Infinity Stones and the Infinity Gauntlet,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.980
but what's more important is that he has a
pretty messy history with Thanos and has served

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:08.590
as the Living Tribunal, a cosmic entity that
preserves equilibrium amongst all realities

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.220
in the Marvel Comics Multiverse.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:10.940
The kicker here?

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:15.180
Both Avengers: Endgame and its predecessor,
Avengers: Infinity War, were meant to include

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.760
the Living Tribunal, and Adam himself was
close to being included in Infinity War.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:24.080
So could Marvel be saving Adam for Phase 4
of the MCU, in the hope of folding him into

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.760
the franchise off the back of these atomic
Infinity Stones?

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:27.880
It seems plausible.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.599
Marvel could even bring the Stones back and
introduce Adam all in one movie.

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:36.239
Infinity War and Endgame screenwriters Christopher
Markus and Stephen McFeely have previously

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:39.959
mentioned that the character can't just show
up from nowhere and Marvel would have to,

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.620
quote, "practically make an Adam Warlock movie"
to bring the character into the universe.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.480
But, if it happened, that would be an ideal
moment to revisit the Infinity Stones.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:51.800
Even if a solo movie isn't in the cards for
Adam, however, he's likely to play a pivotal

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.600
role in the MCU moving forward.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:58.200
And if the Infinity Stones never appear in
the MCU again which could be the case at least

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.320
fans won't lose any more sleep wondering just
what the heck happened to them.

